  "verdict": {
    "closable_at_187_5": "conditional",
    "headline": "G0_not_closed",
    "P2_answer": "At 187.5 MHz, one core, <=300 ERAM and <=71,680 LUT6, no single no-clock scheme closes the measured N core-interval gap on its own. The measured 7,476,245-cycle interval decomposes into scan (B + 5*M) 4,580,135 cycles (61.3%), fixed-block emit/replay 2,431,194 (32.5%), stored-block second pass 398,400 (5.3%), per-block overhead 158,561 (2.1%) and a per-abort scan credit of -92,047 (-1.2%). Alone: emit widening (H) reaches 6,260,648 cycles (0.17% short); a 2-position scan with literals-only dual issue (A, conservative) reaches 7,223,675 (13.5% short). The combination A+H reaches 6,008,078 cycles/frame = 2.318 cycles/B (+4.03% margin) and is the only 187.5 MHz path that closes by arithmetic, but its own design envelope spans 5.19M-7.22M for A alone and 3.97M-6.01M for A+H, so it must be prototyped and taken through TD/STA. Clock-only is not a path: 209.6 MHz is 6.6% short and 224.3 MHz leaves a 0.006% margin on a critical path that already has WNS +0.015 ns at 187.5 MHz. Dual core does not change the per-stream rate."
  },
